    "body": "Yes, I made some changes to the API and haven't updated the examples and spec yet. Thanks for the reminder.\r\n\r\nPreviously, navigator.hid.requestDevice returned a Promise that resolves to a single HIDDevice object, or null for no selection. Now, the requestDevice promise resolves to an array of HIDDevice objects or an empty array for no selection.\r\n\r\nThe intent with this change is to better support devices that expose multiple HID interfaces from the same physical device. Previously, a device with multiple HID interfaces would appear as separate items in the device chooser, one item per interface. If an application needed to access multiple interfaces from the same device, the app had to request access to each interface separately, opening a new chooser for each interface. This resulted in a poor user experience.\r\n\r\nAfter this change, all HID interfaces originating from the same physical device (USB or Bluetooth) are coalesced into a single chooser item. Selecting that item grants permission for all HID interfaces originating from the device, and the Promise resolves to the list of HIDDevice objects representing those interfaces.\r\n\r\nExample:\r\n\r\n```\r\nlet devices = await navigator.hid.requestDevice({filters:[]});\r\nlet device = devices[0];\r\nawait device.open();\r\n```\r\n\r\nI'll close this issue once I've updated the examples and spec.\r\n\r\nAlso, to reply to your previous question about report IDs (apologies for missing that):\r\n\r\nReport ID is an 8-bit identifier defined by the USB HID specification. HID devices support three types of reports: input, output, and feature. If a device needs to support multiple reports of any of these types, the device can assign separate report IDs for each report. For instance, a device that supports both keyboard and mouse inputs might send keyboard data with report ID 1 and mouse data with report ID 2.\r\n\r\nReport IDs are optional, some devices don't use them. For WebHID, pass reportId=0 for devices that don't use report IDs.\r\n\r\nSee the USB HID spec for more information:\r\n\r\nhttps://www.usb.org/document-library/device-class-definition-hid-111",
    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
